# A program as instructions for a robot: every step exact, nothing left out, in an order where each
# step has what it needs.
lesson coding-instructions-for-a-snack v=1 format=teach grade=2 unit=1 subject=coding levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Instructions for a snack"
  goal "Write the steps for a snack so a robot can follow them exactly, in order, with nothing missing."

  look {
    scene 23x22 {
      art pic asset="kitchen-counter" at=canvas(1, 1)
      program prog code=["Take two slices of bread", "Spread jam on one slice", "Put the other slice on top", "Cut the sandwich in half"] title="Pip's sandwich" below=pic gap=1
    }
    say """
      At the lamp rocks Pip is teaching the lamp's robot to make snacks. A robot does exactly what
      each step says, one step at a time, from the top. It cannot guess, so every step has to say
      exactly what to do, the steps have to come in an order where each one has what it needs, and
      no step can be left out.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice coding.snack-first-step count=1 seed=6 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    level easy {
      worked coding.snack-order s=0 level=medium
    }
    practice coding.snack-order count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=7
    practice coding.snack-after-step count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=7
    practice coding.snack-missing count=2 seed=15 easy-seed=8 hard-seed=1
    practice coding.snack-exact count=2 seed=3 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    level hard {
      show coding.snack-wrong-order r=3
    }
    practice coding.lamprocks-sandwich-steps count=1 seed=7
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show coding.snack-wrong-order r=0 easy-r=2 hard-r=4
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show coding.snack-fewest-lines k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=3
  }
  remember {
    say "A robot follows each step exactly and in order, so every step must be exact and none left out."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that a program is a list of exact steps, done in order, and that a robot has nothing
    but those steps to go on. The wrong ideas to watch for are steps a person would understand but a
    robot could not (a bit, nice, enough), and leaving out a step because a person would do it
    without being told. In the working, look for the child acting out each step exactly as written.
    The two-star question finds two lines the wrong way round by reading the program from the top;
    the three-star question uses a repeat for the steps done each time and keeps the steps done once
    outside it. A child who is stuck can play the robot while you read the steps aloud, doing only
    what they say, and go back to the way in; stay on this lesson another day if steps are still
    vague. Off the page, make a real snack together from steps the child writes, following them
    exactly.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the first step is for a bowl of cereal, the order to number is worked once for a jam sandwich before toast is asked, the plate is asked after two lines, the missing steps are the first steps of hot cocoa and a fruit bowl, the vague steps are plainly vague (make it nice, sort out the toast), the robot makes two sandwiches, the two lines the wrong way round are a boiled egg's last two, and the shortest program is for toast, with nothing done once."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the first step is for a bowl of cereal, the order to number is for hot cocoa, the plate is asked after three lines, the missing steps are in the middle, where one wrong choice is a step the program already has, the vague steps sound exact (enough, the right amount, until it is done, the usual time), porridge's lines 1 and 3 are found the wrong way round before the two-star, where a boiled egg's lines 2 and 4 are, the robot makes four sandwiches, and the shortest program has a step done once at each end."
  }
}
